{
  "abstract": "We combine actual zero-source geometry with the ordered-moment method to prove that the limiting lower proportion of zeros of the Riemann zeta function that are both simple and on the critical line is at least 0.67385611. The numerical improvement over the previous 0.673856 bound is small; the contribution is a simultaneous spectral comparison that retains geometric observation and the distance needed by cubic stability. Compression outside the actual inner span of the nonsimple source has zero signed contribution. Interlacing therefore constructs one bounded positive comparison with a cubic constraint, a distance estimate, and an explicit overlap allowance. A separated selection of bounded-depth sites supplies observation through Hardy-space sampling and certified individual-column estimates. Polynomial approximation accounts for crowded cliques, while a counting argument covers the remaining sites. Convexly combining the capacity and distance estimates avoids assigning the same remainder twice. Every signed source term, including arbitrarily deep pairs and all multiplicities, remains in the operator. An explicit zero-density estimate prices only omitted positive credits. Smooth lower and upper physical windows transfer ordered moments to the exact certified window by a Hilbert–Schmidt compression identity. Exact rational inputs and Arb computations at 192 and 256 bits certify the scalar consequence and distinguish the geometric improvement from a norm refinement available to the previous method. The stronger aggregate-observer and small-clique omission questions remain open, as does the Riemann hypothesis.",
